WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.199
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.522 --> 00:00:06.230
DINO CHIESA: Let's
have a look at the API

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.300
runtime in Apigee Edge.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.430
You have services or
you're developing services.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.090
The goal is to expose
them as consumable APIs

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:17.840
with the appropriate security
and behavior and management.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.850
Let me show you just
some of the options

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.310
in Apigee Edge that
help with that.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:25.175
First, let's talk
about API security.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.010
We'll start with a bare API.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:33.460
This is a JSON API, and
there's no security implemented

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.590
by the back-end system.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.840
With Apigee Edge,
you can add security

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.900
without changing
that back-end system.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.765
The simplest and easiest way
to apply security to an API

00:00:43.765 --> 00:00:46.330
is with API key verification.

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:47.840
Let me show you.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.840
I create an API proxy,
specifying the back-end URL

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.140
as the target.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:01.600
I add in key verification with
the Verify API Key policy.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.320
Save that.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.120
And now, every inbound request
will need to pass and API key.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.020
Easy.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.390
By the way, to
make this feasible,

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.090
obviously, we need clients
to invoke the proxy,

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.700
not the existing
back-end system.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.240
And we need to restrict
access to that back-end system

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.990
so that only the Apigee Edge
proxy can connect with it.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:28.480
We normally do this with a
two-way TLS on that connection.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.480
As a more advanced
alternative, Apigee Edge

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.810
supports OAuth version 2.0.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.420
This allows you to verify
a token within the API

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.270
proxy rather than a key.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:45.790
A token is a time-limited key
derived from the consumer key.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:48.430
Virtually every
Apigee customer uses

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.220
OAuth to secure their APIs.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.970
Token verification works very
similarly to key verification.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:58.210
It's a single
policy in the proxy.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.140
Save it.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:03.700
And now, every inbound request
must present a valid token.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.070
Apigee Edge can, of
course, issue tokens

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.130
according to the various
OAuth 2.0 grants--

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.350
client credentials,
authorization code,

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.750
implicit password.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.017
And you can specify the expiry
of tokens, the scope on tokens,

00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:21.850
when you issue them.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.850
Now, let's talk
about API beauty.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.340
We know that beauty is in
the eye of the beholder.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.080
But let me tell you,
today's developers

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.650
don't want to
behold XML or SOAP.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.740
Your older services
might work great,

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.270
but if they're not
easy to consume,

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.330
you won't get the
agility you want.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.420
With Apigee Edge, you can
transform those older services

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.800
into elegant, easy-to-use APIs.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.900
First, let me show
you a SOAP service.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.290
The response includes lots of
angle brackets, namespaces,

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:58.160
and framing.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.080
Not beautiful.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.390
This is what we see
with legacy services

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:07.010
and some third-party SaaS
services you might depend on.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:10.640
An Apigee Edge proxy can accept
a simpler inbound payload

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.610
and transform that
into the heavier XML

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.190
the existing service expects.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:18.980
And at the same time, it
can transform the response

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.640
into lighter weight XML.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.510
An easy way to do
this is with XSLT.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.640
For the request
and the response,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.230
I just include an XSL
policy in the API proxy

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:36.810
and specify the transform.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.150
Let's see it work.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.410
Wow, much simpler.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.200
Taking that a step
further, Apigee

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.890
can transform the
simpler XML into JSON.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:53.820
We use the built-in XML
to JSON policy for that.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.370
Here's what it looks like.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.590
I'll invoke it again.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:03:59.430
So easy.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:02.460
No more angle brackets.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.640
What if I want to transform
the resulting JSON?

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.710
Maybe I want to
rename the properties

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.950
or augment that payload with
some additional client-specific

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.640
data.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.329
I can do that with
a JavaScript policy.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:17.490
This is really neat.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:21.060
Within the API proxy, I can
add a little bit of script

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.670
that manipulates the
payload in any way I'd like.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:28.310
Super easy, simple,
and it works great.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.530
By composing
multiple policies, I

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.780
can have the proxy
perform the transformation

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.060
that I want for any API.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.100
Existing services get renewed.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.360
That's an API makeover.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.430
We talked about
security and beauty.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.350
Let's talk about
performance now.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.840
Some back-end services
are expensive.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.960
Maybe it's a third-party service
where you pay by the call

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.210
or where you've
got a call limit,

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.850
or maybe it's a large data
set that changes slowly,

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.420
like a product catalog, and
it's just not worth reassembling

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.430
that result each time.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:05.460
You can use Apigee Edge
caching to cache the results

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.990
of back-end services.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.230
This can deliver noticeable
performance gains

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.810
for clients and users
and can relieve pressure

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.430
on back-end systems.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:15.990
Just one additional policy.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.930
Here, I'll show you the
difference in performance

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.510
using the Apigee Edge cache.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:28.090
The uncached service takes
hundreds of milliseconds.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:30.850
The cached service
takes less than 10.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:33.200
At scale, that makes
a huge difference.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.710
And with Apigee Edge,
you have fine control

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:38.725
over the cache expiry
and granularity.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.980
OK, I've showed you how to
make a back-end service more

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.130
consumable by adding
security and beautifying it.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.460
Another common desire
is to be able to compose

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.210
multiple back-end systems
into a single API.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.460
Maybe you want to
add a geocoding API.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.940
Maybe you want to add in
a data protection API.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.370
So composition is a thing.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.800
With Apigee Edge,
you can do that.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.830
The service callout
policy is a simple way

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:09.360
to gather a response
that can then

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.260
be composed with the
response from the back end.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.110
I just drop in a
policy, configure it,

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.060
and then I can
combine that response

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.800
with the response
from the target.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:24.680
Suppose you've got
multiple systems

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.650
and you want some light
orchestration, maybe

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:29.990
coordinated with
conditional logic.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.300
With Apigee Edge, you
can use the hosted target

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.570
to do that orchestration.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:39.650
Write your logic in
Node.js or Python or Java.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.600
The inbound request is
proxied to your code.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.290
And that code can then
decide what back end or back

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.030
ends to invoke.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:50.420
It all runs within the scope
of the Apigee API proxy.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:56.910
Finally, Apigee Edge includes
extensions, so you can easily

00:06:56.910 --> 00:07:00.660
connect into existing services,
like Google BigQuery, Cloud

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:05.100
functions or storage, or AWS
Lambda, or the Informatica

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:06.900
Cloud.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.570
The extension capability
is available right

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:12.150
from the policy palette.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:15.090
Just drop in an extension,
and it will execute

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:16.260
within the proxy context.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:22.190
You can combine any or
all of these techniques

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:28.020
in a single API proxy to compose
your APIs the way you'd like.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.210
Now, I want to show you
how you can use Apigee

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.610
Edge for conditional routing.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:34.980
In the simple case,
Apigee Edge gives you

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.300
circuit breaker capability.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.510
Apigee can automatically
distribute load

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:41.190
across a set of
back-end services

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.890
while monitoring the
health of those services

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:47.400
and removing unhealthy
points from rotation.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.680
But suppose you want to
perform some conditional

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.250
routing, maybe routing based
on a varying weight for A/B

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.060
testing or canary
rollouts or maybe routing

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.280
based on a client ID.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.750
Apigee Edge allows
you to do that, too.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:03.840
Here, I built a
dynamic router based

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.510
on a routing table lookup
and some JavaScript

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:09.150
to select the target.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:12.340
When I want to add in a new
target or change the weights,

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.130
I just update the table.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:17.100
No need to touch the
API proxy, and we've

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.740
got data-driven routing.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:22.170
And again, you can
compose this capability

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.040
with the other
elements you've seen,

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:27.510
for example, conditionally
executing an extension callout.

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:33.080
Here, I showed you some
of the basic capabilities

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:34.460
of Apigee Edge--

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:38.030
adding security to existing
APIs, beautifying existing

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:43.520
APIs, composition,
extensions, caching, routing.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:44.900
This is just the start.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:47.360
There's much more
capability in the product--

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.870
role-based access control,
mandatory flows for APIs,

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:52.940
IP whitelisting,
header injection.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.790
Apigee Edge is a power
tool for curating

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:58.640
beautiful, secure,
consumable APIs.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

